<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nidwalden</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Canton of Nidwalden</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nidwalden is a canton of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-lucerne">Lake Lucerne</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stans">Stans</a>; together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obwalden">Obwalden</a> it formed the old canton of Unterwalden. In September 1798 it rose against the constitution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a> and was crushed by French troops, who killed several hundred inhabitants and burned villages. It remains a separate canton of the Swiss Confederation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nidwalden.jpg" alt="Nidwalden" width="320" height="328" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tschubby · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Kanton_Nidwalden_2010.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nidwalden lies in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Lake Lucerne bounds it on the north and mountain chains of the Urner Alps on the other sides, and it borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obwalden">Obwalden</a>, Lucerne, Schwyz, Uri and Bern. It is one of the smallest cantons, with an area of 276.1 square kilometres, of which about 40 per cent is inhabited or used for farming. Forests cover about a third of the canton, and about a quarter is mountain or glacier. Its eleven municipalities include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stans">Stans</a>, Hergiswil and Buochs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest settlement dates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, with sites near Stansstad from 4000 to 3100 BCE, and a La Tène grave of a ten-year-old girl has been found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stans">Stans</a>. In Roman times the valleys were inhabited by a Gallo-Roman or Celtic population, whose names survive in many towns, rivers and mountains. By the 8th century the Alemanni entered the valleys and intermingled with them, and a church was built at Stans.</p><p>By the late 13th century the major powers in Nidwalden were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, Murbach Abbey and Engelberg Abbey. In 1291 Rudolph of Habsburg bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obwalden">Obwalden</a> from Murbach Abbey, and in response the people of Nidwalden, with Obwalden joining shortly before the document was signed, allied with Uri and Schwyz, an alliance regarded as the foundation of the Old Swiss Confederacy. Institutionalised assemblies and courts appeared towards the end of the 14th century. The two halves of Unterwalden met together in the 14th and 15th centuries, but were never really one: Obwalden, for example, took no part in the annexation of Bellinzona, Riviera and Blenio.</p><p>The ideas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> were unpopular in Nidwalden, where many resented the limits placed on freedom of worship by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a> of 1798. French troops attacked the canton on 9 September 1798, badly damaged its infrastructure and killed at least 400 people. After Napoleonic rule ended in 1814 most of the changes were reversed. Nidwalden adopted a new constitution only in 1877, and abolished its open assembly, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a>, in 1997.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nidwalden is a canton of the Swiss Confederation, traditionally counted as a half-canton, with its seat of government and parliament at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stans">Stans</a>. About three quarters of its people were Catholic at the 2000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and most speak German. Agriculture, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and dairy farming, dominated the economy until the middle of the 20th century; tourism centres on the lake and on resorts such as Klewenalp, Stanserhorn and Bürgenstock.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nidwalden” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Glarus and Appenzell Innerrhoden still hold their Landsgemeinden in the open air each spring, while <b>Nidwalden</b>, Appenzell Ausserrhoden and Obwalden abolished theirs in the 1990s; in 2007 the Glarus assembly made the canton the first in Switzerland to let 16-year-olds vote.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The valleys south of Lake Lucerne, Obwalden around Sarnen and <b>Nidwalden</b> around Stans, were rural communities under scattered lordships, including the Habsburgs.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-unterwalden">Canton of Unterwalden</a></li><li><blockquote>But French requisitions, conscription and the suppression of resistance in <b>Nidwalden</b> made it unpopular, and the capital moved to Lucerne and then Bern.</blockquote><a href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
